<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fateh Singh Ahluwalia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1784">1784</a>–<a href="/years/1837">1837</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fateh Singh Ahluwalia was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> ruler of Kapurthala and head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahluwalia-misl">Ahluwalia misl</a> from 1801 until his death. He was a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, exchanging turbans with him as a sign of brotherhood and fighting in his campaigns that built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>. Fearing Ranjit Singh's power, he later sought British protection for his lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sutlej-river">Sutlej</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fateh-singh-ahluwalia.jpg" alt="Fateh Singh Ahluwalia" width="249" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nanrhe10 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fateh_Singh_Ahluwalia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Fateh Singh took over the Ahluwalia Misl after his father, Bhag Singh Ahluwalia, was killed at Phagwara, and seized Phagwara and Banga from Chaudhri Chuhar Mal. Known for his peaceful nature, he concentrated on keeping his own lands, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> was rising as the strongest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> sardar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. Encouraged by his adviser Chaudhri Qadir Bakhsh, he chose friendship with Ranjit Singh, and the two young leaders swore an oath to preserve their bond.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Fateh Singh joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>'s expeditions to the Cis-Satluj region in 1806 and again the following year. On the second, Ranjit Singh helped him recover Naraingarh from Kanwar Kishan Singh of Nahan: after three weeks of hard fighting, which cost the life of the commander Fateh Singh Kalianwala, the fort fell, and its estate, worth Rs. 12,580 a year, went to Fateh Singh. Ranjit Singh later granted him Raikot and Jagraon as well.</p><p>He went with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> to Rawalpindi in 1811 to meet Shah Mahmud of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a>, helped seize the Faizullapur territories in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bist-doab">Jalandhar Doab</a> that year, and fought at the Battle of Haidru on 13 July 1813 against Fateh Khan, the Kabul wazir, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attock">Attock</a>. He also served against Bhimbar, Rajauri, Bahawalpur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and Mankera, often at his own expense. After Jodh Singh Ramgarhia's death in 1816, however, he watched Ranjit Singh seize the Ramgarhia states, refused a share of the spoils and came to fear his ally's expansion.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a> took a summer house Fateh Singh was building near Kapurthala for a fort and summoned him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>. Fateh Singh delayed, and when two battalions under Anand Ram Pindari marched on Kapurthala in December 1825 he fled to Jagraon, in British territory. The British Agent at Ludhiana advised Ranjit Singh against confiscating Kapurthala, and in 1826 Fateh Singh returned, keeping his lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bist-doab">Jalandhar Doab</a> but giving up others west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beas">Beas</a>. He died in October 1837. The historian Lepel Griffin wrote that &quot;English influence barely sufficed to save Sardar Fateh Singh.&quot; After his death the misl became the Kapurthala state.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fateh Singh Ahluwalia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor <b>Fateh Singh Ahluwalia</b> was an ally of Ranjit Singh.</blockquote><a href="/state/kapurthala-state">Kapurthala State</a></li></ul></section>
